# candor effect policy for ebman — `cargo candor policy .candor/policy`
#
# These are the boundaries ARCHITECTURE.md already claims. Until now
# nothing enforced them: the doc says "`ui` reads `app`, `app` calls
# `aws`, `aws` knows about neither", and the only thing keeping that
# true was that nobody had broken it yet.

# The render layer takes &App and returns a frame. It must not reach
# the network, spawn a process, or talk over IPC — a draw path that
# calls AWS turns a repaint into a round trip, and repaints happen on
# every keystroke.
# The invariant that actually matters: no draw path reaches AWS or the
# LLM. A repaint happens on every keystroke, so a render function that
# calls out turns a redraw into a round trip. `:explain` is an
# operator-initiated command, never a side effect of drawing.
deny Net Llm Fs Env  ui

# The AWS boundary knows nothing about the TUI.
forbid aws -> ui

# NOT `forbid aws -> app`, though the layering doc implies it: the
# layer matcher is a prefix match, so `app` also matches
# `aws_sdk_elasticbeanstalk::…::application_name` and the rule fired
# on nine honest EB calls. A rule that cries wolf nine times teaches
# people to skip the output.

# NOT `deny Exec Ipc ui` either — and this one is a real finding, not
# a false positive. `ui::shell::draw_shell` renders the embedded SSM
# pane, which reads a live PTY: Exec to spawn it, Ipc to read it. That
# is the one render path that legitimately is not pure, and it should
# be declared rather than quietly true. If the shell pane ever moves
# behind a channel, this becomes `deny Exec Ipc ui` and the exception
# goes away.
#
# Worth knowing: the SYNTACTIC backend (`candor-scan`) does not see
# this — it reports `ui::draw` with no Exec/Ipc at all. Only the
# type-resolved dylint lint finds it, which is why the CI gate runs
# the lint rather than gating on a scan report.

# `Fs` and `Env` joined the deny list once they became true. candor
# found `ui::overlays::draw_form` reaching both through
# `config::config_path` -> `util::config_dir`, to render the "file: …"
# banner — resolving `$HOME` on every repaint. The banner is computed
# at form-open now (`Form::banner_for`), and the render layer reaches
# neither. That is the intended shape for this file: a finding
# becomes a rule once it is fixed, so the fix can't quietly come back.
#
# NOT asserted here, deliberately:
#
#   deny Clipboard / Net in tests
# Same cause, worse ratio: with `--include-tests` candor reports 102
# test functions reaching Clipboard because it sees the
# `#[cfg(not(test))]` arm of `yank`, which is exactly the arm that is
# compiled out under test. A gate firing 102 times on day one gets
# switched off. The `cfg(test)` stub plus its guard test is what
# actually holds that boundary.
